<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Package sources for this repository — plans/00001, work item 5.

  ⭐ THE SOURCE MAPPING IS WHAT MAKES A CLEAN CLONE WORK WITHOUT CREDENTIALS.
  The GitHub Packages feed needs a PAT for every read, even though the packages themselves are
  PUBLIC (they inherit the repository's visibility; measured after the first publish). Requiring
  a token and being private are independent, and only the first is true here. It is only queried for
  Bennewitz.Ninja.AgentForge.* and Bennewitz.Ninja.LayeredEditors.* — the eleven shared
  libraries — and those ids are only referenced when UseSharedPackages=true. In the default
  development mode nothing asks for them, so the feed is never contacted and a developer with
  no PAT never sees a 401.

  ⚠ Without the mapping, NuGet queries EVERY source for EVERY package, and an unauthenticated
  private feed answers 401 for Avalonia and Serilog too. That would make the whole repo
  un-restorable without credentials, which is the outcome `UseSharedPackages` being a mode
  exists to avoid.

  ⛔ CREDENTIALS NEVER GO IN THIS FILE. A developer adds them to their USER-level config with
  `dotnet nuget update source github`, passing their GitHub username and a PAT carrying
  read:packages; GitHub Actions supplies GITHUB_TOKEN instead. See AGENTS.md for the exact
  command, which cannot be written here because an XML comment may not contain a double dash.

  ⚠ Bennewitz.Ninja.AutoVersioning is PUBLIC and comes from nuget.org — verified from its
  .nupkg.metadata, not assumed. Its id must therefore NOT match the private patterns below,
  which is why they name the two families rather than the whole Bennewitz.Ninja prefix.

  ⛔ AND THAT IS WHY Bennewitz.Ninja.JsonC IS LISTED BY EXACT ID. It belongs to neither family,
  having been renamed out of AgentForge.* because nothing in it knows what an agent is. The
  broad `Bennewitz.Ninja.*` pattern that would cover it automatically is unavailable: it would
  also capture AutoVersioning, and the private feed would then be queried for a package every
  project references, returning 401 on any clone without a PAT.

  ⚠ This file is the FOURTH place a shared library's name has to be known — after the reference
  switch in Directory.Build.targets, PackageMetadataTests, and AssemblyLayeringTests. Nothing
  couples them. The JsonC rename missed this one, and the symptom was NU1101 "no packages exist
  with this id" naming only nuget.org, with the real feeds listed under "were not considered" —
  which reads like a missing package rather than a mapping gap. Only the package canary catches
  it; a normal build never asks for these ids at all.

  ⓘ <clear /> drops any inherited sources so a restore here resolves the same way on every
  machine. If you rely on an internal mirror, add it to this file rather than to your user
  config, and give it a mapping.
-->
<configuration>
    <packageSources>
        <clear />
        <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
        <!-- Written by scripts/package-canary.ps1. Absent on a fresh clone, which is fine:
             nothing maps to it until UseSharedPackages=true. -->
        <add key="localfeed" value="artifacts/localfeed" />
        <add key="github" value="https://nuget.pkg.github.com/JanusMael/index.json" protocolVersion="3" />
    </packageSources>

    <packageSourceMapping>
        <packageSource key="nuget.org">
            <package pattern="*" />
            <!-- plans/00005: the seven ids that replace LayeredEditors live ONLY on nuget.org.
                 These patterns change nothing today, because nothing else matches them and the
                 catch-all above already routes here. They exist because mapping is
                 most-specific-wins: a future broad pattern on github, such as Bennewitz.Ninja.*,
                 would otherwise capture these ids and fail against a feed that never held them.
                 No dot before the AppServices star, so the bare Bennewitz.Ninja.AppServices id
                 matches as well, mirroring that repository's trusted-publishing policy. -->
            <package pattern="Bennewitz.Ninja.AppServices*" />
            <package pattern="Bennewitz.Ninja.ScopedEditors.*" />
        </packageSource>
        <packageSource key="localfeed">
            <package pattern="Bennewitz.Ninja.AgentForge.*" />
            <package pattern="Bennewitz.Ninja.LayeredEditors.*" />
            <package pattern="Bennewitz.Ninja.JsonC" />
        </packageSource>
        <packageSource key="github">
            <package pattern="Bennewitz.Ninja.AgentForge.*" />
            <package pattern="Bennewitz.Ninja.LayeredEditors.*" />
            <package pattern="Bennewitz.Ninja.JsonC" />
        </packageSource>
    </packageSourceMapping>
</configuration>
